<TopicPage slug="subjects/visual-arts/foundational/value-colour-composition">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Every image — cave painting or cinema frame — is built from the same small set of levers, and the
    artist's craft is knowing which lever produces which effect. The three biggest:
    <strong>value</strong> (how light or dark), <strong>colour</strong> (which hue, how intense) and
    <strong>composition</strong> (where the viewer's eye is sent). Beginners chase colour;
    professionals chase value. Squint at any masterwork and it becomes a grey-scale pattern — if that
    pattern reads clearly, the image works at any size, in any light, at the back of a gallery. This
    lesson builds the full visual vocabulary — the elements (value, line, shape, form, texture,
    colour) and the principles that organise them (composition, space, balance, contrast) — then the
    working methods that turn vocabulary into images: recording from observation, and the
    reflect–refine cycle that separates a study from a finished work.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="colour as a conversation, 350 years long — physics, perception and paint trading ideas" widget="timeline" config={colourTimeline} />

  <h2>Value and tone: the structure layer</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Value</strong> — the lightness or darkness of a mark, independent of its hue — is the
    strongest lever, because the human visual system reads light–dark structure before it reads
    anything else: edge detection, depth perception and object recognition all run on luminance.
    A strong image usually has a clear <strong>value plan</strong> — a small number of big light/dark
    shapes (the classic discipline: no more than four or five values at the thumbnail stage), and the
    fastest drill is <strong>notan</strong>, the Japanese light–dark design study: compose with only
    black and white, no grey allowed, until the two-value pattern alone is satisfying. Value also
    carries <strong>form</strong> — the illusion of three dimensions on a flat surface. A sphere is
    nothing but a value gradient: highlight, light, halftone, core shadow, reflected light, cast
    shadow. The old masters' <strong>chiaroscuro</strong> ("light–dark") is value orchestration at
    full power: Caravaggio drowned his compositions in darkness and let a single light source carve
    the figures out of it — the darkness is not empty, it is the stage the light performs on.
    <em>Tone</em> is the word for value used tonally — the continuous gradations between black and
    white — and it is tone that models drapery, skin and weather.
  </p>

  <h2>Line, shape and form: the drawing vocabulary</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Line</strong> is the most human of the elements — nothing in nature is drawn — and it
    does three jobs at once. It <em>describes edges</em> (a contour drawing), <em>directs
    movement</em> (a diagonal pulls the eye faster than a horizontal), and <em>expresses
    temperament</em>: a nervous broken line and a calm continuous line carry different feelings
    before any subject is named. <strong>Shape</strong> is two-dimensional — the flat silhouette a
    shape makes — and shapes divide into <em>geometric</em> (circle, square — human, ordered) and
    <em>organic</em> (irregular, found in growth and weather). <strong>Form</strong> is shape given
    volume: a circle becomes a sphere, a rectangle a box, and the trick that converts one to the
    other is value plus edges (soft edges recede, hard edges advance). <strong>Texture</strong> is
    the surface quality, real (paint thickness — <em>impasto</em>) or implied (hatching that suggests
    fur, stone, water), and implied texture is one of drawing's great economies: a few directional
    marks persuade the eye to manufacture fur from nothing. The relationships among these do the
    heavy lifting: a composition is largely an arrangement of shapes whose silhouettes must read at a
    glance — which is why drawing teachers say "design the shadow shapes, and the object takes care
    of itself".
  </p>

  <h2>Colour: hue, value, chroma — and how colours change each other</h2>
  <p>
    Colour is three decisions pretending to be one, which is the insight behind Munsell's
    <strong>colour tree</strong> (1905) and every modern system since: <strong>hue</strong> (which
    colour — red, teal, ochre), <strong>value</strong> (light or dark) and <strong>chroma</strong> or
    saturation (how vivid or grey). Naming all three independently is what makes colour teachable:
    "mustard" is yellow, mid-value, low-chroma, and knowing that lets you mix it deliberately. The
    relationships that do perceptual work: <strong>complementary pairs</strong> (opposite on the
    wheel — orange/blue, red/green) create maximum vibration side by side and neutralise each other
    when mixed; <strong>temperature</strong> — warm colours advance, cool recede — is the painter's
    depth cue, running the atmosphere in every landscape from Turner onward; and <strong>limited
    palettes</strong> (three or four tubes, mixed) produce harmony because every colour on the canvas
    shares the mixing DNA. The timeline's central experiment belongs to Michel Eugène Chevreul
    (1839), chemist at the Gobelins tapestry works, called in because dyes that looked right on the
    sample looked wrong in the tapestry: his discovery — <strong>simultaneous contrast</strong> — is
    that adjacent colours change each other (a grey next to red looks greenish; two identical squares
    differ on different backgrounds), a property of perception, not pigment. The Impressionists
    painted by it: Monet's shadows are purple <em>because</em> the light is yellow, placing
    complements side by side so the eye does the mixing. Goethe's <em>Theory of Colours</em> (1810)
    had already insisted colour is how the eye and mind respond, not just wavelengths — the poet and
    the physicist were both partly right, and painting lives in the overlap. (The 1996 sRGB note is
    the modern edge of the same story: screens can display only a fraction of the colours the eye
    distinguishes, which is why the sunset never quite prints.)
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> shadows are not "black versions of the object" and white is not
  the absence of colour. Shadows are cooler, more saturated and more colourful than beginners paint
  them (compare a photo), and in pigment <em>black plus colour</em> is usually dead — value is
  lowered with the colour's complement or with raw umber. The squint test exists for exactly this:
  if the value pattern works, almost any hue choices will survive; if it doesn't, no colour saves it.</p>

  <h2>Space, perspective and scale</h2>
  <p>
    The picture plane is flat; depth is manufactured, and the manufacturing has three engines.
    <strong>Linear perspective</strong> — codified in Florence (Brunelleschi's demonstrations c.
    1420s, Alberti's <em>On Painting</em> 1435) — has parallel lines converge on vanishing points and
    equal sizes shrink with distance: an invention, not a fact about vision, and Renaissance Europe's
    most consequential one (Japanese prints, Chinese landscapes and most of human image-making get
    depth without it). <strong>Aerial (atmospheric) perspective</strong> uses what the air does:
    distant forms lose contrast, cool toward blue and soften — Leonardo's smoky mountains are the
    textbook. <strong>Overlap and scale</strong> finish the job: nearer objects occlude farther ones,
    and the viewer's sense of a figure's size comes entirely from context — a doorway, a tree, a
    horizon line. <strong>Scale</strong> inside the frame is also an expressive tool: an oversized
    figure in a small room reads as psychological pressure; the tiny pilgrims in a vast Alpine
    landscape read as human smallness (Friedrich built whole careers on the ratio).
  </p>

  <h2>Composition: balance, space, and the sequence of looking</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Composition</strong> is the directing layer: contrast, edges and leading lines steer the
    eye in a deliberate order — the artist composes the <em>sequence of looking</em>, not just the
    picture. Its instruments: <strong>focal contrast</strong> (the area of greatest value or colour
    contrast is where the eye lands first — place your subject where you want attention spent);
    <strong>leading lines</strong> (roads, arms, gaze directions — the viewer enters where the
    strongest line begins); <strong>balance</strong> (symmetrical balance is formal and stable;
    asymmetrical balance — a small dark mass countering a large light one, like a scale with a coin
    against a feather — is where the liveliness lives); <strong>negative space</strong> (the shapes
    between things are compositional objects in their own right; Rabier/Reinhardt-style figure-ground
    drills — and the classic goblet/two-faces illusion — show that the eye must be able to read
    either); and <strong>edge control</strong> (sharp edges attract, soft edges release — masters
    sharpen one area and deliberately lose the rest). The famous recipes — the rule of thirds, the
    golden ratio, Itten's colour contrasts — are <strong>training wheels, not physics</strong>. Great
    artists break all of them: Caravaggio centres and drowns; Hokusai flattens space entirely in
    <em>The Great Wave</em> and it may be the most composed image ever printed. The rule exists to
    be <em>broken knowingly</em>: first make the image that follows the principle, then decide what
    breaking it buys you.
  </p>

  <h2>Recording and refining: the working cycle</h2>
  <p>
    Vocabulary becomes skill through two working methods the syllabus names explicitly.
    <strong>Recording from first-hand and secondary resources:</strong> first-hand means drawing from
    life — the still object on the table, the figure, the street — because observation teaches what
    no photograph pre-solves (photos flatten, auto-expose and already "chose" the values); secondary
    means reference — museums, photos, other artists — used deliberately for what you cannot access.
    The <strong>sketchbook</strong> is the instrument of both: quick observational studies, colour
    notes ("shadow on snow: blue-violet, not grey"), copies of masterworks copied <em>analytically</em>
    (why did Vermeer put the light there?), and thumbnails. <strong>Reflecting, refining and
    adapting</strong> is the cycle that converts studies into work: step back (literally — across
    the room, or in a mirror, or upside down, all of which defeat the eye's local adaptation),
    compare the image against the intention, diagnose which lever failed (value? composition? the
    colour temperature of the shadow?), and revise. Every professional painter works this loop —
    thumbnails before commitment, a value study before colour, colour studies before the final —
    because the loop is cheap and the final canvas is not. The habit is the skill: an artist is
    someone who has run the reflect–refine cycle enough thousands of times that the first guesses
    are already good.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    A few layers for the committed. <strong>Colour constancy is the brain's edit:</strong> the visual
    system re-balances the whole scene toward its assumed light source, which is why a white page
    looks white indoors and out, and why painted colour must be judged in the painting's own imagined
    light — a snow shadow is painted blue-violet and <em>seen</em> as white. <strong>Gamut mapping
    and limited palettes are the same idea</strong> across media: choosing the subset of colours the
    piece will live in (Zorn's palette; a film's LUT) buys harmony at the price of range, and the
    sRGB gamut question — which colours does this display even have? — is gamut choice applied to
    your audience. <strong>Value grouping is the master's diagnostic:</strong> posterise any admired
    image to four levels and count the big shapes; the count is usually small, which is the actual
    secret the squint test measures. <strong>And the elements are culturally inflected, not
    universal:</strong> Western art history's perspective and modelling is one spatial grammar among
    several — Japanese ukiyo-e flat planes and expressive line, Islamic art's geometric pattern and
    calligraphic line, Chinese landscape's shifting perspective — which is why the timeline above is
    a Western conversation worth having and worth leaving: the levers are the same, the grammars
    differ, and the artist's real education is learning enough grammars to know which one a given
    image is speaking.
  </p>
